/*!
@defgroup i2c
@ingroup mod_communication Low Power Inter-Integrated Circuit (I2C)
@brief Hardware I2C bus controller supporting master and slave operation modes.

@details
The I2C module provides a low-power Inter-Integrated Circuit (I2C) controller
that supports efficient communication on an I2C bus as a master and/or a slave.
It is interrupt-driven and supports both blocking and non-blocking transfer APIs,
with optional DMA acceleration for high-throughput applications.

@section i2c_features Key Features
- Master and slave operation on the same peripheral instance.
- Interrupt-based transfer engine with blocking and non-blocking API variants.
- 7-bit and 10-bit slave addressing.
- Configurable baud rate with automatic prescaler calculation.
- Supports multiple I2C speed modes (device-dependent):
  - Standard-mode (Sm): up to 100 kbit/s
  - Fast-mode (Fm): up to 400 kbit/s
  - Fast-mode Plus (Fm+): up to 1 Mbit/s (if `I2C_HAS_FAST_PLUS_MODE`)
  - High-speed mode (Hs): up to 3.4 Mbit/s (if `I2C_HAS_HIGH_SPEED_MODE`)
  - Ultra-fast mode (UFm): up to 5 Mbit/s, unidirectional (if `I2C_HAS_ULTRA_FAST_MODE`)
- Hardware FIFO for both transmit and receive paths.
- DMA support for master and slave transfers (`I2C_USING_DMA`).
- Slave always-listening mode with event-driven callbacks.
- Slave on-demand mode for command-initiated transfers.
- Repeated START condition support for chained master transfers.
- Line-low timeout detection for bus error recovery.

@section i2c_modes Supported Operating Modes

| Mode             | Enum Value            | Max Speed     | Direction      | Condition Macro               |
|------------------|-----------------------|---------------|----------------|-------------------------------|
| Standard (Sm)    | I2C_STANDARD_MODE     | 100 kbit/s    | Bidirectional  | Always available              |
| Fast (Fm)        | I2C_FAST_MODE         | 400 kbit/s    | Bidirectional  | Always available              |
| Fast-plus (Fm+)  | I2C_FASTPLUS_MODE     | 1 Mbit/s      | Bidirectional  | `I2C_HAS_FAST_PLUS_MODE`     |
| High-speed (Hs)  | I2C_HIGHSPEED_MODE    | 3.4 Mbit/s    | Bidirectional  | `I2C_HAS_HIGH_SPEED_MODE`    |
| Ultra-fast (UFm) | I2C_ULTRAFAST_MODE    | 5 Mbit/s      | Unidirectional | `I2C_HAS_ULTRA_FAST_MODE`    |

@section i2c_arch Module Architecture

The I2C module is organized in a two-layer architecture:

@verbatim
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                  Application Code                    │
│           (calls I2C_DRV_* functions)                │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│           I2C Driver Layer (i2c_driver.h/c)          │
│  ● Instance-based API (I2C_DRV_MasterInit, etc.)     │
│  ● Separate master / slave init and data paths       │
│  ● Blocking and non-blocking transfer functions      │
│  ● DMA and interrupt transfer modes                  │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│       I2C HW Access Layer (i2c_hw_access.h)          │
│  ● Direct register-level access (inline functions)   │
│  ● Master and slave control / status / data I/O      │
│  ● Clock, FIFO, interrupt, and DMA configuration     │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│        I2C IRQ Routing Layer (i2c_irq.h/c)           │
│  ● Maps hardware IRQ vectors to driver handlers      │
│  ● Device-series specific (YTM32B1L/B1M/B1H)        │
├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│             I2C Peripheral Hardware                  │
│   (MCTRL, MSTS, MDATA, SCTRL, SSTS, SDATA, etc.)   │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
@endverbatim

@subsection i2c_arch_driver Driver Layer (i2c_driver.h)
Application code should **only** use the `I2C_DRV_*` functions from i2c_driver.h.
These functions accept a zero-based **instance** index and resolve the hardware
base address internally using `I2C_BASE_PTRS`. Master and slave modes are
initialized independently and can coexist on different instances.

@subsection i2c_arch_hw HW Access Layer (i2c_hw_access.h)
This internal layer provides low-level inline register accessors. It is
organized into functional groups for both master and slave sides:
- **Master**: enable/reset, clock config, pin/NACK config, FIFO, data I/O,
  status, interrupt, DMA, timeout
- **Slave**: enable, address config, status, ACK/NACK control, clock stretching,
  data I/O, interrupt, DMA, high-speed mode detection

@section i2c_config Configuration Parameters

@subsection i2c_config_master i2c_master_user_config_t Fields

| Field           | Type                    | Description                                                       | Default Value          |
|-----------------|-------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------|
| slaveAddress    | uint16_t                | Target slave address (7-bit or 10-bit).                           | 32                     |
| is10bitAddr     | bool                    | If true, slaveAddress is interpreted as 10-bit.                   | false                  |
| operatingMode   | i2c_mode_t              | I2C speed mode (Standard / Fast / etc.).                          | I2C_STANDARD_MODE      |
| baudRate        | uint32_t                | Desired SCL frequency in Hz.                                      | 100000                 |
| baudRateHS      | uint32_t                | High-speed SCL frequency (only if `I2C_HAS_HIGH_SPEED_MODE`).    | N/A                    |
| masterCode      | uint8_t                 | Master code for Hs-mode (0-7, only if `I2C_HAS_HIGH_SPEED_MODE`).| N/A                    |
| transferType    | i2c_transfer_type_t     | Transfer engine: I2C_USING_INTERRUPTS or I2C_USING_DMA.           | I2C_USING_INTERRUPTS   |
| dmaChannel      | uint8_t                 | DMA channel number (ignored if not using DMA).                    | 0                      |
| masterCallback  | i2c_master_callback_t   | Optional callback invoked from ISR at transfer end.               | NULL                   |
| callbackParam   | void *                  | User parameter passed to masterCallback.                          | NULL                   |

@subsection i2c_config_slave i2c_slave_user_config_t Fields

| Field           | Type                    | Description                                                       | Default Value          |
|-----------------|-------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------|
| slaveAddress    | uint16_t                | Own slave address (7-bit or 10-bit).                              | 32                     |
| is10bitAddr     | bool                    | If true, slaveAddress is interpreted as 10-bit.                   | false                  |
| operatingMode   | i2c_mode_t              | I2C speed mode.                                                   | I2C_STANDARD_MODE      |
| slaveListening  | bool                    | If true, slave is always listening after init.                    | true                   |
| transferType    | i2c_transfer_type_t     | Transfer engine: I2C_USING_INTERRUPTS or I2C_USING_DMA.           | I2C_USING_INTERRUPTS   |
| dmaChannel      | uint8_t                 | DMA channel number (ignored if not using DMA).                    | 0                      |
| slaveCallback   | i2c_slave_callback_t    | Callback for slave events (TX_REQ, RX_REQ, STOP, etc.).          | NULL                   |
| callbackParam   | void *                  | User parameter passed to slaveCallback.                           | NULL                   |

@section i2c_usage Usage Patterns

@subsection i2c_usage_master_basic Pattern 1: Master Blocking Send and Receive

The most common use case: initialize in master mode, send data to a slave,
then receive a response using blocking calls.

@code{.c}
#define I2C_INST  (0U)

i2c_master_state_t masterState;
i2c_master_user_config_t masterConfig;
uint8_t txData[] = { 0x00, 0x01, 0x02, 0x03 };
uint8_t rxData[4];

/* Step 1: Get default master configuration */
I2C_DRV_MasterGetDefaultConfig(&masterConfig);
masterConfig.slaveAddress = 0x50U;
masterConfig.baudRate = 400000U;

/* Step 2: Initialize I2C master */
I2C_DRV_MasterInit(I2C_INST, &masterConfig, &masterState);

/* Step 3: Send data (blocking, with STOP) */
I2C_DRV_MasterSendDataBlocking(I2C_INST, txData, sizeof(txData), true, 100U);

/* Step 4: Receive data (blocking, with STOP) */
I2C_DRV_MasterReceiveDataBlocking(I2C_INST, rxData, sizeof(rxData), true, 100U);

/* Step 5: De-initialize */
I2C_DRV_MasterDeinit(I2C_INST);
@endcode

@subsection i2c_usage_master_nonblocking Pattern 2: Master Non-Blocking Transfer

Start a transfer and poll for completion, or use a callback for notification.

@code{.c}
#define I2C_INST  (0U)

i2c_master_state_t masterState;
i2c_master_user_config_t masterConfig;
uint8_t txData[] = { 0xAA, 0xBB };
uint32_t bytesRemaining;

I2C_DRV_MasterGetDefaultConfig(&masterConfig);
masterConfig.slaveAddress = 0x50U;
I2C_DRV_MasterInit(I2C_INST, &masterConfig, &masterState);

/* Start non-blocking send */
I2C_DRV_MasterSendData(I2C_INST, txData, sizeof(txData), true);

/* Poll for completion */
while (I2C_DRV_MasterGetTransferStatus(I2C_INST, &bytesRemaining) == STATUS_BUSY)
{
    /* Optionally do other work */
}

I2C_DRV_MasterDeinit(I2C_INST);
@endcode

@subsection i2c_usage_master_chained Pattern 3: Master Chained Transfers (Repeated START)

Use the `sendStop` parameter to keep the bus active for a register-read
operation (write register address, then read data).

@code{.c}
#define I2C_INST  (0U)

i2c_master_state_t masterState;
i2c_master_user_config_t masterConfig;
uint8_t regAddr = 0x10U;
uint8_t rxData[2];

I2C_DRV_MasterGetDefaultConfig(&masterConfig);
masterConfig.slaveAddress = 0x50U;
I2C_DRV_MasterInit(I2C_INST, &masterConfig, &masterState);

/* Send register address WITHOUT stop (sendStop = false) */
I2C_DRV_MasterSendDataBlocking(I2C_INST, &regAddr, 1U, false, 100U);

/* Read data WITH stop (sendStop = true) — repeated START is generated */
I2C_DRV_MasterReceiveDataBlocking(I2C_INST, rxData, sizeof(rxData), true, 100U);

I2C_DRV_MasterDeinit(I2C_INST);
@endcode

@warning When `sendStop` is set to false, the master holds the SCL line low
after the transfer. The application **must** follow up with another transfer
(with `sendStop = true`) to release the bus. Failure to do so will block
the entire I2C bus indefinitely.

@subsection i2c_usage_slave_listening Pattern 4: Slave Always-Listening Mode

In this mode, the slave is armed at initialization and responds to master
requests via the callback function.

@code{.c}
#define I2C_INST  (0U)

i2c_slave_state_t slaveState;
i2c_slave_user_config_t slaveConfig;
uint8_t slaveTxData[4] = { 0x10, 0x20, 0x30, 0x40 };
uint8_t slaveRxData[4];

void slaveCallback(i2c_slave_event_t event, void *param)
{
    (void)param;
    switch (event)
    {
        case I2C_SLAVE_EVENT_TX_REQ:
            I2C_DRV_SlaveSetTxBuffer(I2C_INST, slaveTxData, sizeof(slaveTxData));
            break;
        case I2C_SLAVE_EVENT_RX_REQ:
            I2C_DRV_SlaveSetRxBuffer(I2C_INST, slaveRxData, sizeof(slaveRxData));
            break;
        case I2C_SLAVE_EVENT_STOP:
            /* Transfer complete */
            break;
        default:
            break;
    }
}

/* Initialize slave in listening mode */
I2C_DRV_SlaveGetDefaultConfig(&slaveConfig);
slaveConfig.slaveAddress = 0x50U;
slaveConfig.slaveListening = true;
slaveConfig.slaveCallback = slaveCallback;
I2C_DRV_SlaveInit(I2C_INST, &slaveConfig, &slaveState);

/* Slave is now actively listening — no further action needed */
@endcode

@subsection i2c_usage_slave_ondemand Pattern 5: Slave On-Demand Mode

In this mode, the slave is commanded to transmit or receive through explicit
API calls. The actual moment of data exchange depends on the I2C master.

@code{.c}
#define I2C_INST  (0U)

i2c_slave_state_t slaveState;
i2c_slave_user_config_t slaveConfig;
uint8_t rxData[8];

I2C_DRV_SlaveGetDefaultConfig(&slaveConfig);
slaveConfig.slaveAddress = 0x50U;
slaveConfig.slaveListening = false;
I2C_DRV_SlaveInit(I2C_INST, &slaveConfig, &slaveState);

/* Block until the master sends data to this slave */
I2C_DRV_SlaveReceiveDataBlocking(I2C_INST, rxData, sizeof(rxData), 5000U);

I2C_DRV_SlaveDeinit(I2C_INST);
@endcode

@section i2c_api API Quick Reference

@subsection i2c_api_master_init Master Initialization & De-initialization
| Function                          | Description                                              |
|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| I2C_DRV_MasterInit()              | Initialize I2C master with user configuration.           |
| I2C_DRV_MasterDeinit()            | De-initialize I2C master, release resources.             |
| I2C_DRV_MasterGetDefaultConfig()  | Fill master config struct with default values.           |

@subsection i2c_api_master_config Master Configuration
| Function                          | Description                                              |
|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| I2C_DRV_MasterSetBaudRate()       | Set SCL frequency and operating mode at runtime.         |
| I2C_DRV_MasterGetBaudRate()       | Read the currently configured SCL frequency.             |
| I2C_DRV_MasterSetSlaveAddr()      | Change the target slave address at runtime.              |

@subsection i2c_api_master_xfer Master Data Transfer
| Function                              | Description                                          |
|---------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| I2C_DRV_MasterSendData()              | Start non-blocking transmit to slave.                |
| I2C_DRV_MasterSendDataBlocking()      | Blocking transmit to slave with timeout.             |
| I2C_DRV_MasterReceiveData()           | Start non-blocking receive from slave.               |
| I2C_DRV_MasterReceiveDataBlocking()   | Blocking receive from slave with timeout.            |

@subsection i2c_api_master_ctrl Master Transfer Control
| Function                              | Description                                          |
|---------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| I2C_DRV_MasterAbortTransferData()     | Abort an in-progress non-blocking transfer.          |
| I2C_DRV_MasterGetTransferStatus()     | Check transfer status and remaining bytes.           |

@subsection i2c_api_slave_init Slave Initialization & De-initialization
| Function                          | Description                                              |
|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| I2C_DRV_SlaveInit()               | Initialize I2C slave with user configuration.            |
| I2C_DRV_SlaveDeinit()             | De-initialize I2C slave, release resources.              |
| I2C_DRV_SlaveGetDefaultConfig()   | Fill slave config struct with default values.            |

@subsection i2c_api_slave_buf Slave Buffer Management
| Function                          | Description                                              |
|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| I2C_DRV_SlaveSetTxBuffer()        | Provide transmit buffer (used in listening mode).        |
| I2C_DRV_SlaveSetRxBuffer()        | Provide receive buffer (used in listening mode).         |

@subsection i2c_api_slave_xfer Slave Data Transfer
| Function                              | Description                                          |
|---------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| I2C_DRV_SlaveSendData()               | Start non-blocking slave transmit (on-demand mode).  |
| I2C_DRV_SlaveSendDataBlocking()       | Blocking slave transmit with timeout.                |
| I2C_DRV_SlaveReceiveData()            | Start non-blocking slave receive (on-demand mode).   |
| I2C_DRV_SlaveReceiveDataBlocking()    | Blocking slave receive with timeout.                 |

@subsection i2c_api_slave_ctrl Slave Transfer Control
| Function                              | Description                                          |
|---------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| I2C_DRV_SlaveAbortTransferData()      | Abort an in-progress slave transfer.                 |
| I2C_DRV_SlaveGetTransferStatus()      | Check slave transfer status and remaining bytes.     |

@section i2c_notes Important Notes

@subsection i2c_notes_clock Clock & Pin Dependencies
- The I2C protocol clock must be enabled via the @ref clock_manager **before**
  calling I2C_DRV_MasterInit(). The driver does not manage clocks internally.
- I2C pins must be routed to the I2C module via the PORT configuration before
  use. Refer to the pin routing HAL for pin mux settings.

@subsection i2c_notes_irq Interrupt Configuration
- The driver enables the I2C module interrupts internally, but interrupt
  **priority** configuration must be done by the application via the
  @ref interrupt_manager.

@subsection i2c_notes_baudrate Baud Rate Accuracy
@warning Due to hardware prescaler limitations, the actual baud rate may differ
from the requested value. Always call I2C_DRV_MasterGetBaudRate() after
I2C_DRV_MasterSetBaudRate() to verify the actual frequency configured.

@subsection i2c_notes_sendstop Repeated START and sendStop
@warning When using `sendStop = false` in a master transfer, the bus is not
released after the transfer. The application **must** follow with another
transfer that has `sendStop = true`. Failing to do so will hold SCL low
indefinitely, blocking the entire I2C bus.

@subsection i2c_notes_abort Abort Limitations
@warning Aborting a master reception with I2C_DRV_MasterAbortTransferData()
is **not supported** due to hardware behavior — the module will continue a
started reception even if the FIFO is reset.
@warning When aborting a master transmission, the user must ensure that the
address byte has already been sent before calling the abort function.

@subsection i2c_notes_listening Listening Mode Timing
- In always-listening mode (`slaveListening = true`), I2C_DRV_SlaveInit()
  must be called **before** the master begins the transfer.
- In on-demand mode (`slaveListening = false`), both the init function and
  the corresponding send/receive function must be called before the master
  starts the transfer on the bus.

@subsection i2c_notes_unsupported Unsupported Features
- Fast-plus, High-speed, and Ultra-fast modes may not be available on all
  devices. Check `I2C_HAS_FAST_PLUS_MODE`, `I2C_HAS_HIGH_SPEED_MODE`, and
  `I2C_HAS_ULTRA_FAST_MODE` macros for device support.

@section i2c_integration Integration Guide

@subsection i2c_integration_files Compilation Units
The following source files must be added to the project build:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/i2c/i2c_driver.c
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/i2c/i2c_irq.c
@endverbatim

@subsection i2c_integration_include Include Paths
Add the following directories to the toolchain include path:
@verbatim
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/inc/
${SDK_PATH}/platform/drivers/src/i2c/
@endverbatim

@subsection i2c_integration_symbols Compile Symbols
No special compile-time symbols are required for this module. Device-specific
feature macros (`I2C_HAS_*`) are automatically defined in the device feature
headers.

@subsection i2c_integration_deps Dependencies
- @ref clock_manager — Required for enabling the I2C peripheral clock.
- @ref osif — Required for semaphore-based blocking transfers.
- @ref interrupt_manager — Required for enabling/disabling I2C interrupts.
- @ref dma_driver — Required when using DMA transfer mode.

*/
